#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Prove the gate's tests can actually fail.

For each mutant below: apply the edit, run the WHOLE unit suite, and require that the NAMED test
goes red. Every module, not just :core -- a mutant in an at-rest encoding is proved by a :data
test, and a harness that only ran one module could not tell a vacuous test there from a real one.
A mutant that leaves the suite green means nothing in the suite observes that behaviour, and the
test that claims to cover it is decoration. A mutant that reddens a different test than the one
named means the coverage is not where it is documented to be.

Every source file is restored afterwards, including on Ctrl-C or an exception.

Mutants are exact source text, which is brittle: editing a targeted line makes its mutant STALE,
which happened five times while round 32 was being written. That is the deliberate trade, and the
five catches are the argument for it. STALE is reported as NOT PROVEN and exits non-zero, so
brittleness costs a script edit and can never quietly claim coverage that is not there. Do not
"fix" it by loosening the match: a fuzzy mutant that still applies after the code moved is a mutant
that is no longer testing what its name says.

    tools/mutation-check            # all mutants
    tools/mutation-check battery    # only mutants whose id contains "battery"
"""
